// Distributed schedule, THREE compute workers — the worker-COUNT
// robustness cell (TASK-0367). A row-band partition of the outer `i`
// axis across {w0, w1, w2}; host runs load/save.
//
// Why this cell exists
// --------------------
// Every other multi-worker schedule in the tree uses the identical
// workers={host,w0,w1,w2,w3} shape (N=4 compute workers). The
// partition-decompose + transfer/gather codegen was therefore proven
// for exactly one worker count. 16-jacobi/distributed already
// exercises an UNEVEN 4-worker partition (interior 6 rows / 4 →
// bands 2,2,1,1), so "uneven at N=4" is covered. The genuinely
// untested dimension this cell adds is the worker COUNT itself: a
// 3-worker partition. See TASK-0367 (cycle-213 strategic-analysis
// finding F1).
//
// Partition shape
// ---------------
// `loop i : partition=workers;` on the outer axis. N=16 rows, 3
// workers. The floor-with-spillover policy (TASK-0262,
// `compute_partition_bands`) gives base = 16/3 = 5, extras = 16 % 3
// = 1 → the first worker gets 6 rows, the other two get 5:
//
//     w0: i = 0..6   (6 rows)
//     w1: i = 6..11  (5 rows)
//     w2: i = 11..16 (5 rows)
//
// This is BOTH uneven (the spillover residual lands on w0) AND a
// worker count != 4 — exercising the residual policy under a new
// geometry. The decompose helper is worker-count-agnostic by
// construction (no 4 / power-of-2 / even-division assumption
// anywhere), so this cell needs NO compiler change.
//
// Why this is bit-identical with naive/distributed (no new reference)
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// The k-accumulator semantics are unchanged under the i partition:
// each output c[i][j] is the SAME sum over k = 0..N regardless of
// which worker computes row i, or HOW MANY workers there are. Workers
// are independent on i; no halo, no cross-worker carry, no reduction
// across i. The existing 07-matmul/reference.bin oracle therefore
// applies verbatim — exactly as the 4-worker distributed.sched.nuc
// header states. No new reference.bin, no algorithm/kernel change.
//
// Axis-mapping (TASK-0301) is worker-count-independent
// ----------------------------------------------------
// `b` is indexed [k][j] (not by i) → the per-symbol filter excludes i
// from b's tile bounds → empty bounds → whole-array broadcast of b.
// `a` (indexed [i][k]) and `c` (indexed [i][j]) carry the per-worker
// i_band slice on their tiles. None of that depends on the worker
// count — it is the SAME filter the 4-worker cell uses.
//
// Why sync transfers
// ------------------
// Sync-only schedule lowers on every tier-1 backend without
// capability gaps (same rationale as distributed.sched.nuc). Async
// variants are out of scope for this coverage cell.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0, w1, w2 };

    place load_a on host;
    place load_b on host;
    place save_c on host;
    place madd   on { w0, w1, w2 };

    // Row-band partition the outer i axis. N=16, 3 workers →
    // floor-with-spillover bands 6, 5, 5 (uneven AND N != 4).
    loop i : partition=workers;

    // All sync transfers. a is row-banded by i, b is whole-array
    // broadcast (not indexed by i), c is row-banded by i on gather.
    transfer a : sync;
    transfer b : sync;
    transfer c : sync;
}
